lesson g3-function-machines v=1 format=puzzles grade=3 unit=9 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Function machine puzzles"
  goal "Work out what comes out, what went in, and what the machine does."

  puzzle stars=1 {
    show machine.one-step r=4 n=7 easy-r=3 easy-n=3 hard-r=8 hard-n=9
  }
  puzzle stars=1 {
    show machine.backwards r=6 n=8 easy-r=3 easy-n=4 hard-r=7 hard-n=12
  }
  puzzle stars=1 {
    show algebra.balance-equation a=12 b=9 easy-a=6 easy-b=4 hard-a=19 hard-b=15
  }
  puzzle stars=1 {
    show puzzle.pyramid
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show machine.find-the-rule r=6 n=4 easy-r=3 easy-n=2 hard-n=8
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show machine.two-step a=3 b=4 n=6 easy-a=2 easy-b=1 easy-n=3 hard-a=6 hard-b=9 hard-n=9
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show machine.two-step-back a=4 b=5 n=7 easy-a=2 easy-b=1 easy-n=3 hard-a=5 hard-b=9 hard-n=9
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show algebra.both-sides a=14 b=9 c=17 easy-a=11 easy-b=6 easy-c=12 hard-a=19 hard-b=14 hard-c=18
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    practice logic.always-sometimes count=1 seed=5
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    practice logic.counterexample count=1 seed=2
  }
  puzzle stars=3 {
    show algebra.two-same n=9 c=7 easy-n=4 easy-c=3 hard-n=15 hard-c=12
  }
  puzzle stars=3 {
    show machine.find-two-step a=3 b=2 x=2 y=5 z=9 easy-a=2 easy-b=1 easy-z=7 hard-a=4 hard-b=5 hard-z=10
  }
  puzzle stars=3 {
    show puzzle.triangle-big x=13 y=24 z=18 easy-x=11 easy-y=12 easy-z=14 hard-x=27 hard-y=28 hard-z=29
  }

  grown-ups """
    Running the machine backwards is the hard one, and it is the one that turns into algebra. If
    the child is stuck, ask what undoes multiplying. With two machines the last one is undone
    first, like taking off shoes before socks. The balance puzzles can be done by adding up both
    sides, but ask whether there is a quicker way. In the secret rule puzzle, look at how much the
    number out grows when the number in grows by one. In the bigger sum triangle, trying a number
    for A and checking it against all three boxes is exactly the right method. Two puzzles ask
    whether a claim about numbers is always, sometimes or never true, and which number shows a
    claim is wrong: the example the child found matters more than the word they circled, so ask
    for it.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the machines multiply small numbers (3 times 3, 12 back to 4, 3 times 2), the scales hide 4 and then 7, the pyramid's bricks are under 7, the two-step machines take 3 in, the claim and Kai's number come from the plainer ones, the two boxes hide 4, the table's machine doubles and adds 1, and the circles add to 37."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the machines multiply large numbers (8 times 9, 84 back to 12, 6 times 8), the scales hide 15 and then 13, the pyramid's bricks are 10 to 20, the two-step machines take 9 in with the largest rules, the claim and Kai's number are the ones that take working out, the two boxes hide 15, the table's machine multiplies by 4 and adds 5, and the circles add to 84."
  }
}
